In the study of computer architecture, one recurring theme is the tension between generality and specialization. A general-purpose processor must handle an enormous variety of workloads reasonably well, while a specialized accelerator can achieve much higher efficiency on a narrow class of problems by discarding flexibility it does not need. Matrix multiplication sits squarely at the center of this tradeoff for modern machine learning workloads, because so much of the arithmetic in a transformer model reduces to dense linear algebra: projecting activations through learned weight matrices, expanding and contracting hidden dimensions inside feed-forward blocks, and computing attention scores between queries and keys. On mobile graphics processors, this arithmetic competes for the same execution units and memory bandwidth that also serve rendering workloads, so any technique that reduces the number of instructions needed per multiply-accumulate operation tends to pay for itself many times over. Cooperative matrix extensions expose exactly this kind of instruction-level efficiency by letting a whole subgroup of invocations cooperate on loading, multiplying, and storing tiles of a matrix using a small number of specialized instructions instead of many scalar operations. The benefit is largest when the tiles align cleanly with the natural granularity of the hardware, and smallest, or even negative, when a workload's shapes are irregular enough that padding or fallback paths dominate the cost. Establishing a trustworthy baseline before adopting such an extension is therefore not a formality; it is the only way to know whether an observed speedup reflects the extension itself or simply reflects noisy, unrepeatable measurement. In the study of computer architecture, one recurring theme is the tension between generality and specialization. A general-purpose processor must handle an enormous variety of workloads reasonably well, while a specialized accelerator can achieve much